</path>
</svg>
    </button></div>
</div>
<p>If your job submission receives an error, you will need to address the i=
ssue and resubmit your job. If no error is received, your job has entered t=
he queue and will start when resources are available. See <a class=3D"refer=
ence internal" href=3D"https://rcdsdocs.it.northwestern.edu/systems/quest/u=
ser-guide/slurm/slurm.html?utm_source=3Dchatgpt.com#managing-jobs">Slurm Co=
mmands and Job Management</a> to learn how to get information about your jo=
b.</p>
</section>
</section>
<section id=3D"job-scheduling-and-fairshare">
<span id=3D"fairshare"></span><h2>Job Scheduling and Fairshare<a class=3D"h=
eaderlink" href=3D"https://rcdsdocs.it.northwestern.edu/systems/quest/user-=
guide/slurm/slurm.html?utm_source=3Dchatgpt.com#job-scheduling-and-fairshar=
e" title=3D"Link to this heading">#</a></h2>
<p>The Slurm job schedule allocates submitted jobs to available resources b=
ased on several factors, resulting in an overall priority score.</p>
<p>Each Slurm account on Quest has a <strong>fairshare</strong> score that =
determines the relative priority of jobs submitted as a part of that accoun=
t. The fairshare score is used to prioritize jobs when more resources have =
been requested than are available.  If the job queue is empty and compute r=
esources are available, regardless of your fairshare score, your jobs will =
run. You will never run out of compute hours in this model.</p>
<p>Job priority is affected by:</p>
<ul class=3D"simple">
<li><p><strong>Recent Use of Quest</strong>: The fairshare score changes ov=
er time based on recent past use of Quest.  If you or other members of your=
 Slurm account used large amounts of resources over the past few weeks, the=
 priority of current jobs will be lower. The contribution of past resource =
usage to priority calculations decays over time. Without new usage, the fai=
rshare scores are restored significantly within a month.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Resource Type</strong>: General Access Research I compute re=
sources have lower starting fairshare scores, and thus priority, than Resea=
rch II compute resources. This is because Research II resources are provide=
d for research projects requiring more total computing time; Research II re=
sources receive a greater share of the system.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Job Age</strong>: Age is length of time an eligible job has =
been waiting in the queue. Jobs will accumulate priority proportional to th=
eir age.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Partition</strong>: For some Priority Access compute resourc=
es, job priority varies based on the <a class=3D"reference internal" href=
=3D"https://rcdsdocs.it.northwestern.edu/systems/quest/user-guide/slurm/slu=
rm.html?utm_source=3Dchatgpt.com#partitions">partition</a> that the job use=
s.</p></li>
</ul>
<p>If you are using a General Access compute resource for your jobs, the fa=
irshare scores of those jobs will be affected by the overall compute time u=
sage by everyone who has access to that particular compute resource (i.e. S=
lurm account). If you are using a Priority Access resource that has its own=
 compute nodes, your own usage of those nodes will be the determining facto=
r for your job=E2=80=99s fairshare score and priority relative to other use=
rs of your Priority Access compute resource.</p>
<p>For more detailed information about Slurm=E2=80=99s multi-factor priorit=
y system, see the <a class=3D"reference external" href=3D"https://slurm.sch=
edmd.com/priority_multifactor.html" target=3D"_blank">Slurm documentation <=
svg version=3D"1.1" width=3D"1.0em" height=3D"1.0em" viewBox=3D"0 0 16 16" =
aria-hidden=3D"true" style=3D"display: inline-block; vertical-align: middle=
; fill: currentColor;"><path d=3D"M3.75 2h3.5a.75.75 0 0 1 0 1.5h-3.5a.25.2=
